## Migration: ASP.NET Web Forms → ASP.NET Core 8

Your code uses two patterns that rely on `Thread.Abort` internally:

| .NET Framework Pattern | Issue in Modern .NET |
|---|---|
| `Response.End()` | Calls `Thread.Abort` — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | The `true` (endResponse) triggers `Thread.Abort` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code in .NET 6+ |

---

### Migrated ASP.NET Core Controller

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // Export: Return a FileResult — no Response.End() needed.
    // The framework handles content-disposition and streaming.
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // File() sets Content-Disposition automatically
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv");
    }

    // Redirect: Return RedirectResult — no Thread.Abort, no catch block.
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult ProcessAndRedirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch — it doesn't exist in .NET 8
    }

    private string GenerateReport() { /* ... */ }
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### Key Differences & Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---|---|
| `Response.End()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Return an `IActionResult` (`File()`, `Content()`, `Ok()`) — the framework ends the response |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` aborts the thread | Return `Redirect(url)` — execution stops naturally via `return` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` as expected flow | Remove entirely — this is dead code in .NET 8. Use `catch (OperationCanceledException)` only if you need to handle client disconnects via `HttpContext.RequestAborted` |
| `Response.Write()` + manual headers | Use `File()` for downloads, `Content()` for text, or write to `Response.Body` stream directly |
| Auth check with redirect | Prefer `[Authorize]` attribute — the framework handles the redirect to login automatically |

---

### For long-running requests (bonus)

If `ProcessRequest()` is long-running, honor client disconnection:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> ProcessAndRedirectAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // cancellationToken is automatically bound to HttpContext.RequestAborted
    await ProcessRequestAsync(cancellationToken);
    return Redirect("/success");
}
```

This is the cooperative cancellation replacement for the old `Thread.Abort`-on-disconnect behavior.
